   979.447417            5.965560       88.407134   \n2  2018-01-03        739.360168            4.865243       91.774589   \n3  2018-01-04        730.779091            5.000000       91.606061   \n4  2018-01-05        756.418605            5.953488       88.930233   \n\n   total_trips  \n0         1375  \n1         3252  \n2         2857  \n3         3300  \n4         2150  ","content_type":"text/plain"}}},"children":[],"key":"NUHu1jXQDJ"}],"key":"aTrfF3hs35"}],"key":"yRQTb7Nfv8"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Results","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"pMbJLcvlHK"}],"identifier":"results","label":"Results","html_id":"results","implicit":true,"key":"zXsEd8VrR6"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Question 1","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"mB7xpiQdbn"}],"identifier":"question-1","label":"Question 1","html_id":"question-1","implicit":true,"key":"hsYGeyUs9e"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Temperature is positively related to ridership. In the merged data, average temperature has a moderate positive correlation with total trips at about 0.36. (see visualization_analysis.ipynb)\nIn regression, a 1 degree C increase in average temperature is associated with about 270 more trips per day, and the estimate is statistically significant with robust standard errors.(see visualization_analysis.ipynb)\nEven though most days are between about 10 and 17 C, the scatterplot still shows an upward trend, which is a nice reminder that small comfort changes matter in SF. (see visualization_analysis.ipynb)","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"q0hg242T8q"}],"key":"AaQ9VudHrU"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Question 2","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"ecjl4faeSU"}],"identifier":"question-2","label":"Question 2","html_id":"question-2","implicit":true,"key":"BCo84V1iki"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rain reduces ridership. Precipitation has a negative correlation with total trips around minus 0.28.(see visualization_analysis.ipynb)","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"p2j4PdehcA"}],"key":"cnXPOv3x7k"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In regression, 1 mm more rain is associated with about 90 fewer trips per day.(see visualization_analysis.ipynb)","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"dLMQWkizx8"}],"key":"GhewuDx8Oe"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If we instead use a rainy day indicator, rainy days have about 1480 fewer trips on average, even after controlling for day of week.(see visualization_analysis.ipynb)","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"kTSzGklmev"}],"key":"I9tpiy6LMk"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The boxplot also shows a big gap between rainy and dry days, which matches the model. (see visualization_analysis.ipynb)","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"HHMKQv7Q4o"}],"key":"DMqkkAIFJM"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Question 3","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"sv5aNYMCFi"}],"identifier":"question-3","label":"Question 3","html_id":"question-3","implicit":true,"key":"tgHrvM3Mb6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ridership follows a seasonal pattern. Average trips rise through spring and summer, peak around September to October, then fall in winter. (see visualization_analysis.ipynb)","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"vKfnj1bujV"}],"key":"t3nhyEZ0hF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When we split by precipitation buckets, dry days consistently have the highest usage and trips generally increase from 2018 to 2024, with a dip around 2021. (see visualization_analysis.ipynb)","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"ES5n7dA9Xg"}],"key":"Lve2cCpTh0"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limitations","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"IFIthGJ7P4"}],"identifier":"limitations","label":"Limitations","html_id":"limitations","implicit":true,"key":"jHhok5Riij"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is not a causal claim. Weather is not randomly assigned and other factors like commuting patterns, holidays, pricing, station availability, and special events can move ridership. Our simple controls help a bit, but they do not remove all confounding. The model R squared is about 0.17, so a lot of variation is still explained by factors outside temperature and rain. (see visualization_analysis.ipynb)","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"OJB4elNiRF"}],"key":"FaKOtRogD7"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conclusion","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"yjWnhxFmH5"}],"identifier":"conclusion","label":"Conclusion","html_id":"conclusion","implicit":true,"key":"Q0CF4tYldI"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Even in San Francisco’s mild climate, warmer and drier days have meaningfully higher bike usage. Rain has the largest immediate impact and can reduce trips by about 1500 on a typical rainy day.","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"Z1MyU0gY2E"}],"key":"OBwbU5RwGm"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Author Contributions","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"Jr5pob4drU"}],"identifier":"author-contributions","label":"Author Contributions","html_id":"author-contributions","implicit":true,"key":"ifonRoXB1t"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Jun Kang: repository organization, helper functions in src, testing, and main narrative notebook.","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"smhQCQouPi"}],"key":"wEvNTFOpy2"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logan: data cleaning and preprocessing notebook.","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"bgSqr6zTwo"}],"key":"llGpZ3cagD"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":